=======================================================================
LATERALUS STANDARD LIBRARY TUTORIAL — Stack (stdlib/stack.ltl)
=======================================================================

Welcome to the `stdlib/stack` tutorial. In this guide, we explore Last-In,
First-Out (LIFO) data structures in Lateralus, covering construction,
core push/pop/peek operations, edge cases with empty stacks, and advanced
manipulations such as swapping and duplicating elements.

-- 1. Introduction to LIFO Stacks ------------------------------------

A stack is a linear collection that restricts insertion and removal to
one end of the structure, known as the "top". Think of a stack of plates:
the last plate placed on top is the first one removed.

In Lateralus, stacks are built on top of native lists with optimized
helper functions provided by `stdlib/stack.ltl`.

-- 2. Basic Usage: Push, Pop, and Peek -------------------------------

Let's look at a runnable example demonstrating core stack operations
with pinned test vectors.

```lateralus
import stack
import testing

@test
fn test_stack_core_operations() {
    // Create a new empty stack
    let s = stack.new()
    assert_true(stack.is_empty(s), "new stack is empty")
    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 0, "size is 0")

    // Push elements onto the stack
    s = stack.push(s, "first")
    s = stack.push(s, "second")
    s = stack.push(s, "third")

    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 3, "size after 3 pushes")
    assert_false(stack.is_empty(s), "stack is not empty")

    // Peek at the top element without removing it
    assert_eq(stack.peek(s), "third", "peek top element")
    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 3, "size unchanged after peek")

    // Pop elements (LIFO order)
    let res1 = stack.pop(s)
    let val1 = res1[0]
    s = res1[1]
    assert_eq(val1, "third", "first popped is third")

    let res2 = stack.pop(s)
    let val2 = res2[0]
    s = res2[1]
    assert_eq(val2, "second", "second popped is second")

    // Remaining stack should contain only "first"
    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 1, "size is 1")
    assert_eq(stack.peek(s), "first", "top is now first")
}
```

-- 3. Empty-Stack Handling and Edge Cases ----------------------------

Robust error handling is critical when dealing with data structures.
Attempting to `pop` or `peek` from an empty stack in Lateralus safely
returns `nil` or preserves safety without crashing the runtime.

```lateralus
@test
fn test_empty_stack_edge_cases() {
    let s = stack.new()

    // Peek on empty stack returns nil
    assert_eq(stack.peek(s), nil, "peek empty stack returns nil")

    // Pop on empty stack returns [nil, stack]
    let res = stack.pop(s)
    assert_eq(res[0], nil, "pop empty stack value is nil")
    assert_eq(stack.size(res[1]), 0, "pop empty stack preserves empty stack")

    // Drop from empty stack
    let dropped = stack.drop(s, 5)
    assert_true(stack.is_empty(dropped), "drop on empty stack is safe")

    // Swap top on stack with < 2 elements
    s = stack.push(s, 42)
    let swapped = stack.swap_top(s)
    assert_eq(swapped, [42], "swap_top with 1 element is safe")
}
```

-- 4. Advanced Stack Operations ------------------------------------

The stack module also provides convenience helpers like `dup`, `swap_top`,
`drop`, and `fold` for stack-based virtual machine patterns or expression
evaluation.

```lateralus
@test
fn test_advanced_stack_ops() {
    let s = stack.from_list([10, 20, 30])

    // Duplicate top element
    s = stack.dup(s)
    assert_eq(stack.peek(s), 30, "top is duplicated 30")
    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 4, "size is now 4")

    // Swap top two elements (30 and 30 -> swap keeps them equal or swaps distinct)
    s = stack.push(s, 40)
    // stack is now [10, 20, 30, 30, 40]
    s = stack.swap_top(s)
    // top two elements (30 and 40) are swapped -> [10, 20, 30, 40, 30]
    assert_eq(stack.peek(s), 30, "swapped top")

    // Drop top 2 elements
    s = stack.drop(s, 2)
    assert_eq(stack.size(s), 3, "dropped 2 elements")

    // Fold over stack from bottom to top
    let sum = stack.fold(s, 0, fn(acc, x) { acc + x })
    assert_eq(sum, 60, "fold sum of [10, 20, 30]")
}
```

-- 5. Conclusion ----------------------------------------------------

The `stdlib/stack` module provides a clean, idiomatic LIFO interface for
Lateralus programs, suitable for parsers, calculators, graph traversals,
and virtual machines. Run your tests with:

```bash
lateralus test stdlib/stack.ltl
```
